[Music]  [Music]  [Music]  conquering the West required a great  deal of force settling and pacifying  this vast area required a great deal  more violent actions were usually the  prelude to the achievement of social  stability  they've been romanticized for more than  a century in the fiction and folklore of  the West first came war against Mexico  in 1846 to 48 and recurrently through  the 19th century against the Native  Americans the United States even tiptoed  to the brink of war against Britain in  1846 and again in 1859 over where to  draw the boundary between the United  States and Canada on the Pacific coast  before finding a way to resolve the  question diplomatically most of the  western territories endured a phase of  vigilante justice in the years before  attaining statehood during which  suspected criminals were hanged or  flogged by angry mobs hatred of ethnic  Outsiders especially the Chinese was  another source of violence as was  retaliation against theft finally as the  scale of Western mining increased so did  conflict around the mines with strikers  fighting pitched battles against  employers private armies against  Pinkerton detectives and against strike  breakers most sections of the western  frontier was settled disproportionately  by men first with women following later  and gradually redressing the population  imbalance a combination of factors led  to the men being heavily armed first  they were displacing the former  residents usually by force or by  coercive treaty arrangements with the  Indians second they often supplemented  their diet by hunting and had learned as  boys how to shoot  third they bore the memory of their  forefathers the American revolutionaries  who deceived independence through their  willingness to fight and win after the  1860s they also bore the more recent  memory of the Civil War in which great  cities and armies north and south heeded  the call to arms and  and how to use guns and fourth the  generation that settled the plains and  mountains states came of age in the era  of inexpensive mass-produced firearms  notably the Colt revolver and the  Winchester rifle which made the  ownership of weapons easier and cheaper  than ever before foreign travelers to  the West between 1860 and 1900 often  mentioned how many men were armed and  that they displayed a prickly sense of  Honor  backed by bravado and courage these were  of course the kind of conditions that  made violence common and often lethal  settlement of the Far West was slow and  sparse in the early years of the 19th  century though the handful of white  mountain men were themselves masters of  the rifle the sudden rush to California  in 18-49 was something new population  rose they're much faster than the local  authorities ability to deal with the  problems the gold-seekers nearly all men  brought with them these were the perfect  conditions for vigilante justice to  arise in the gold camps hundreds of men  living and working side by side feared  that the gold they found might be stolen  when they discovered a thief they often  created ad hoc courts heard the evidence  and dealt summarily with the offender  the punishment was usually flogging or  banishment but if the offense was more  serious the offender was hanged even so  the mining camps moved as quickly as  they could to formalize legal  institutions create courts and elect  sheriff's travelers who were impressed  by the presence of guns were also  impressed by how men from back east had  brought with them the civic and  Democratic traditions of their homes and  the camps and in the port city of San  Francisco whose population was also  growing at breathtaking speed vigilante  justice was also popular at first  newspaper editors supported it as the  necessary response to what would  otherwise be chaos and brute force  they also appreciated the fact that it  saved the nascent community a lot of  money and time the speed and ferocity of  vigilante punishments was designed to  have the strongest possible deterrent  effect for example an Englishman named  James Stewart traveled from Australia  which was then the site of a large  British penal colony to the gold rush  camps of California he was suspected of  a series of thefts and then of killing a  merchant in 1851 the San Francisco  vigilance committee seized him it was  organized by Sam Brannan a Mormon  businessman and one of the wealthiest  men in the city hundreds of supporters  of the committee poured into the streets  as news spread of Stewart's arrest the  local sheriff lacking manpower to take  the accused villain away from the  vigilantes made a feeble and ineffective  protest before Stewart was marched down  to the harbor and hanged in public  California's governor approved of the  action when an indignant judge impaneled  a grand jury to indict the ringleaders  it refused to do so several of its  members belong to the vigilance  committee a few weeks later the  committee attacked the city jail seized  two other men and hanged them both  despite the authority's attempts to  protect them the committee abolished  itself after three months out of  deference to due process but it  reconvened in 1856 and undertook for  more lynchings again with a high degree  of popular and press support public  demonstrations and pseudo military  regalia the states and territories of  the plains and Mountain West showed  comparable patterns of vigilante justice  well - feared that the gold dust they  were painstakingly accumulating might be  stolen cattle men had to be vigilant  against the threat of horse and cattle  thieves Theodore Roosevelt was  expressing a fairly common opinion when  he wrote that in the early days of these  ventures vigilante justice and even  vendetta's were defensible as soon as  the communities become settled  begin to grow with any rapidity the  American instinct for law asserts itself  but in the early stages each individual  is obliged to be a law to himself and to  God his right with a strong hand in 1863  Bannock a remote community in Montana  territory was plagued by a gang of  hijackers who attacked gold convoys and  stagecoaches  seizing property and killing travelers  the citizens of Bannack and those of the  nearby settlement of Alder Gulch created  a vigilance committee of their own only  to discover that the criminal mastermind  was their own sheriff Henry Plummer  Henry Plummer was an odd many-sided  character extremely hot-tempered who  over the years had shot and killed  several men in barroom brawls originally  from Maine he'd made a fortune in the  California Gold Rush and been a  successful businessman but then gone to  San Quentin prison for murder released  after winning an appeal claiming that  he'd killed his victim in self-defense  Plummer migrated to Montana made a good  impression on the local people and won  election as sheriff as in San Francisco  the Montana vigilantes who organized to  a post Plummer acted in broad daylight  they seized one of his gang George I  eaves and cross questioned him at an  outdoor trial that lasted for three days  despite icy winter weather and was  attended by hundreds of local gold  miners at the end of the trial he was  summarily hanged the committee went on  to round up the other members of the  gang lynching 20 men including Plummer  himself in January 1864 another Montana  vigilance committee led by Granville  Stewart and known as Stewart's  Stranglers hunted down cattle rustlers  across the territory  one of the territory's richest Ranchers  Stewart was enraged by the theft of a  prize stallion and 35 cattle his  vigilantes killed a large number of  thieves variously estimated at between  twenty and a hundred nine men died in  one incident the Battle of Bates  point in 1884 when Stuart's men set fire  to the cabin in which the rustlers were  sheltering and shot them down as they  emerged from the flames  he later justified his ruthless approach  to law and order in a book forty years  on the frontier and he retained a  reputation  not for lawlessness but for being one of  Montana's leading pioneer citizens  President Polk Grover Cleveland made him  the United States ambassador to Uruguay  and Paraguay in neighboring Wyoming the  Johnson County War was one of the more  famous conflicts of the 1880s and early  90s in which both sides accused the  other of theft large cattle ranch owners  said that small ranchers often the  Cowboys whom the large ranchers employed  in the annual roundup  were stealing unbranded or maverick  calves as a way to start herds of their  own obviously a newborn calf is not  branded which creates an opportunity to  claim that it's really a stray or wild  animal that somehow become mixed with  the herd the small farmers responded  that the big men who are monopolizing  the land of the public domain even  though they did not own it both groups  were smarting after the severe winter of  1880 six to seven which had killed tens  of thousands of cattle on the open range  the Wyoming stock Growers Association  representing the large ranchers and able  to dictate rules favorable to themselves  at the territorial legislature finally  hired a group of mercenaries from Texas  they arrived in the area in April 1892  with a target list of men to kill the  local men turned the tables on them  almost at once besieged them in a cabin  and exchanged gunfire for three days  before a troop of US cavalry arrived to  separate the two sides ironically the  local county government was so poor it  could not afford to bring the  mercenaries to trial and most of them  were simply permitted to leave unscathed  small businessmen were also matched  against a big corporation in the muscle  Slough conflict of central California  homesteading farmers in Tulare and  Fresno counties in California's Central  Valley we're told in 1879 that their  claims were invalid and that the land  they were farming actually belonged to  the immensely wealthy and politically  influential Southern Pacific Railroad a  judge found in favor of the railroad but  the homesteaders rather than accept the  verdict fought back in a gun battle of  May 1880 in which seven men was shot  dead five of the surviving settlers  served jail terms were acclaimed as  popular heroes on their release a year  later those who died took on the aura of  popular martyrs the railroad prevailed  in the long run but already unpopular is  sank even further in public esteem it  was the villain of Frank Norrises novel  the octopus published in 1901 when two  bandits Chris Evans and John Sonntag  began robbing trains in the early 1880s  they were acclaimed as popular heroes to  well-known writers of the era Joaquin  Miller and Ambrose Bierce wrote admiring  articles about them in the San Francisco  Examiner as the historian Richard  Maxwell Brown explains the anti railroad  law breaking of Evans and Sonntag was a  surrogate for the seething resentment  towards the Southern Pacific by peaceful  law-abiding Californians the incident  and the publicity it generated had an  effect on Theodore Roosevelt among many  others in his approach to restraining  the power of Monopoly corporations  incidentally train robberies are a stock  theme in western movies the very first  recognizable movie Western was the Great  Train Robbery from 1903 a moment's  reflection will show that in reality  they cannot have been easy to carry out  especially if the train was moving  passenger trains full of men many of  them likely to be armed with dangerous  targets even for gangs who did manage to  bring them to a halt Train Robbery  certainly did happen especially in the  30 years before 1900 the notorious Jesse  James gang  carried out at least four in the 1870s  and Butch Cassidy carried out a famous  train robbery at Wilcox Wyoming in 1899  but statistically they were fairly rare  banks were much easier targets  stationary more likely to be full of  money and more compact no wonder they  were far more often the scene of Western  robberies another frequent scene of  violence was the cow towns of Kansas  where the Texas cattle drives of the  1860s and 70s ended mayhem often ended  when Cowboys rode into town and cut  loose after months of solitude on the  trail the city certainly wanted their  business but knew they had to police the  Texas Cowboys by disarming arresting or  if it came to the point shooting them if  they got out of control while Bill  Hickok was the sheriff of Abilene Kansas  in the early 1870s and once killed a  Texas cowboy named Phil Coe that was the  exception rather than the rule  however the historian Richard white  notes that as the cowboy towns developed  so their sheriff's worked out methods to  encourage a respect for the law and to  restrain violence as he writes although  restricting possession of handguns  remains an emotional issue today  nineteenth-century cattle town residents  resorted to gun control without much  hesitation Wichita and other towns  outlawed the carrying of handguns within  the city limits and established police  forces to patrol the towns and enforce  the ordinances he goes on to note the  extreme rarity of man-to-man gun duels  sheriffs and marshals rarely faced down  gun fighters but they regularly knocked  down and dragged away drunks controlling  and disciplining the inebriated was in  fact their main duty he also notes that  in the atmosphere of the late 1860's and  early 1870s there was some residual  civil war tension in the cow towns the  citizens tended to be former union  supporters while Bill Hickok himself  came from a family of abolitionists  whereas the Texas Cowboys we usually X  confess  Hertz in cases of vigilantism the  perpetrators clearly had an idea of  justice and believed that they could  achieve it only through collective  public action the same was true of the  IRA's periodic race riots in which the  anglo-saxon majority proudly declared  its racial views then attacked members  of an ethnic minority group especially  mexicans Chileans and chinese california  in particular had been the scene of  large-scale Chinese immigration from the  gold rush years onwards many more  immigrants came from China to build the  Central Pacific Railroad in the late  1860's providing a welcome source of  labor to Leland Stanford and the other  railroad entrepreneurs working-class  whites bitterly resented them for the  economic competition they offered for  their willingness to work for low wages  alleging also that they were klemish  addicted to opium dirty and dishonest  the California workingmen's party led by  Denis Kearney was explicitly  anti-chinese as was the supreme order of  Caucasians a white supremacist group  widespread anti-chinese racism led to a  ban on Chinese voting in the California  Constitution of 1879 then the Chinese  Exclusion Act passed by Congress in 1882  the onset of economic hard times the  next year led to further scapegoating of  Chinese workers in 1885 white mine  workers in Wyoming went on strike  against the mines owner the Union  Pacific Railroad in response to a  threatened pay cut when the Chinese  workers of Rock Springs refused to join  them  the whites rampage through the local  Chinatown killed about 30 men and drove  hundreds more out of town and into the  mountains Seattle and Tacoma to  Washington towns on the Pacific coast  also endured anti-chinese riots which  amounted to a campaign of ethnic  cleansing Street mobs forced several  thousand to flee in 1886  ten gold workers in a nearby camp who  tried to stay were murdered the next  year the worst violence in Western  history apart from that of the Indian  Wars involved clasp conflict at metal  ore mines and coal mines mine owners  tended to be tough hard bitten  entrepreneurs with a broad streak of  ruthlessness working conditions were  chronically unsafe in their minds death  from fire from collapsed tunnels and  from gas inhalation were common to make  matters worse the mine owners rarely  paid their workforce in money instead  they paid the man in scrip a kind of  private currency that the workers were  forced to redeem at company owned stores  where prices were high and quality low  in remote mountain districts the workers  also usually lived in company housing  which made them exceptionally vulnerable  to the will of the mine owners they  reacted by creating unions the hardrock  workers who mined metals formed the  Western Federation of miners and the  coal miners formed the United Mine  Workers the unions campaigned for safer  conditions a Czech way man to ensure  that the ore and coal they brought to  the surface was being weighed honestly  an 8-hour workday and collective  bargaining rights for the union's  themselves the owners fought tooth and  nail against the union's fearing loss of  control and loss of profitability if the  state authorities and the local  community supported them the men that  could sometimes win their strikes that's  what happened in [ __ ] Creek Colorado  in 1894 when Governor Davis weight sent  in the militia to safeguard the miners  and when most of the townspeople agreed  with the strikers cause weight belongs  to the radical populist party and at one  election the previous year just as the  Great Depression of the 1890s began he  visited the scene of the strike and  persuaded the owners not to extend the  workday from 8 to 10 hours with no pay  increase as they had in 10  later governor's Republicans and  Democrats more often sided with the mine  owners from inclination and from fear  that strikes would damage their state's  reputation for business governor James  Peabody for example issued court  injunctions and sent in the state  militia to help the mine owners when the  [ __ ] creek miners struck again in  1903  this time the union was defeated many of  its leaders were expelled from the  community and the company hired strike  breakers instead neither did the mine  owners hesitate to infiltrate the  union's with spies and to kidnap beat up  or intimidate particularly dedicated  union leaders state support for mine  owners proved decisive in Idaho to a  trainload of miners dynamited the  factory of the Bunker Hill mining  company in 1899 because the company  refused to recognize the Union the  structure valued at half a million  dollars was completely demolished in the  blast the Union had used dynamite in an  earlier strike of 1892 to destroy an ore  processing plant at the town of gem and  remembered the spectacular effects of  this tactic this time around however the  company retaliated by persuading the  governor Frank's TuneIn burg to call on  the president for federal troops who  arrested nearly a thousand miners they  were herded into enclosures known as  bullpens and left there for months with  no due process some were stranded for  more than a year when a local newspaper  editor criticized the situation his  paper was closed he was accused of  sedition he was forced to join the  suffering men in their vermin-infested  makeshift prison this collective  violation of the miners civil rights  broke the Union one of the miners Harry  orchard later assassinated governor  student big feeling a particular sense  of grievance because student berg had  originally been a populist elected  largely with miners votes  orchard also suspected that the governor  had been bribed by the mine owners to  change sides a sense  a tional trial ensued in which orchard  alleged that he'd been hired by big bill  Haywood and two other leaders of the  Western Federation of miners Haywood was  an anarcho-syndicalist who did believe  in class warfare and was willing to use  violence to smash capitalism he was one  of the founders of the IWW the  Industrial Workers of the world  America's most revolutionary trade union  in the short term however Haywood was  vindicated thanks to the courtroom  performance of his attorney Clarence  Darrow the jury found him not guilty  whereas orchard went to prison for the  rest of his life escaping the death  penalty only because of another  governor's grant of clemency the most  violent episode in the Western mining  patches came in 1914 during a long  winter strike in Ludlow Colorado and  mines owned by the Colorado fuel and  iron company this was an area just north  of the New Mexico line in which the  unions have previously been defeated by  a combination of employer intimidation  use of injunctions and the militia and  by the employment of strike breakers the  strike breakers themselves a decade  after their first arrival on the scene  were now as burdened as the men they had  displaced and finally rebelled against  oppressive working conditions in  September 1913 many of them were recent  immigrants from Italy Greece and the  Slavic lands forced to leave the company  houses in which they were living they  created a tent colony and stayed in it  through the winter  despite bitter temperatures in the  Sangre de Cristo Mountains the company  responded by hiring armed men and  calling out the state militia and a  tense standoff ensued as the strikers  tried to prevent a new generation of  strike breakers from taking their jobs  on April the 20th 1914 the militia fired  a machine gun into the tent colony and  set fire to it at least 30 people were  killed including two women and 11  children asphyxiated by smoke in a  cellar under one of the tents enraged  workers retaliated attacking company  property and militiamen through  the region killing dozens more and  suffering more casualties in return this  was probably the most violent episode on  American soil since the Battle of the  Little Bighorn in 1876 finally President  Woodrow Wilson intervened by sending a  regiment of the United States cavalry to  the area to restore peace the  Congressional hearings that followed  depicted john d rockefeller owner of the  colorado fuel and iron company in a very  unfavorable light Rockefeller had  already been singled out as an  unscrupulous robber baron by the  muckraking journalist Ida Tarbell eager  to repair his reputation and to win  renown for philanthropy rather than  brutality Rockefeller belatedly ordered  the company to improve wages and  conditions at Ludlow instituting an  eight-hour day an outlawing child labor  from this distance it's difficult to  imagine how volatile industrial  workplaces could be in the early 20th  century the workers were enraged at bad  conditions low pay  unscrupulous treatment by their bosses  and chronic insecurity in danger at the  workplace  the owners meanwhile were usually in  tough competition with each other and  shared a common threat from the union's  knowing that some of the Union men would  not hesitate to take direct action they  resisted by hiring labor spies  infiltrators as Jean provocateur and by  using Pinkerton detectives to intimidate  or assault the leaders when Harrison  gray Otis editor of the Los Angeles  Times refused to let his own workforce  unionize and when he published strong  anti-union editorials to radical labor  activists the McNamara brothers  responded by dynamiting the offices of  the newspaper in 1910 killing 21 people  and injuring a hundred more they tried  to kill Otis himself but the bomb they  planted at his home failed to explode  again the case attracted influential men  on both sides including Clarence Darrow  Lincoln Steffens a famous socialist  writer of the era and Eugene Debs  leader of the American Socialist Party  Deb's was more or less obliged to  concede that the McNamara brothers were  guilty but he wrote that the oppressive  conditions of American capitalism had  forced them to take desperate measures  as this brief survey of violence in the  American West shows it was a place where  conflict could erupt suddenly among men  preoccupied with their honor and their  reputation for bravery men who were well  armed and exposed to danger in a newly  settled environment the feeling that  they were being cheated either by  thieves or by rivals for the land by  Chinese immigrants or by industrial  bosses could lead quickly to escalating  violence members of the American elite  did not regard this propensity for  violence as an entirely bad thing since  they too valued the code of bravery and  honor when Theodore Roosevelt raised his  regiment the Rough Riders in 1898 for  the war against Spain in Cuba he  recruited cowboys Indians and sheriff's  men he'd known from his days as a Dakota  wrench man some of our best recruits  came from Colorado he wrote one a very  large hawk-eyed man Benjamin Franklin  Daniel's had been marshal of Dodge City  when that pleasing town was probably the  toughest abode of civilized man to be  found anywhere on the continent  naturally he viewed the dangers of  battle with philosophic calm readiness  to fight what Roosevelt called manliness  seemed to him the essential antidote to  an America that was in danger of losing  the martial virtues and becoming soft on  the other hand certain factors prevented  western violence from ever becoming  chronic the area never deteriorated into  civil war one of these factors was the  tradition of due process already very  strong in American life if the memory of  Lexington and Concord made Americans  willing to reach for their guns the  memory of the founding fathers made them  equally willing to seek legal solutions  to their problems nearly all of them  were literate nearly all were familiar  with filing a claim at the land office  or registering town lots and nearly all  regarded violence only as a fallback  when law appeared inadequate the sheer  size and scale of the West also tempted  men in conflicts to move rather than  stay and fight risking their own lives  and those of their families taking the  long view we should be more impressed by  this willingness to submit to the law  and to cooperate than by the colorful  but transient phases of frontier  lawlessness

